 questions: [
  ["The AHA 2021 regimen for an adult with a prosthetic heart valve and no allergy, having an extraction, is:","Amoxicillin 2 g orally, 30 to 60 minutes before the procedure",["Amoxicillin 3 g orally, 60 minutes before the procedure","Amoxicillin 500 mg orally, three times daily for seven days","Amoxicillin 2 g before and 1 g six hours after the procedure"],"A single 2 g dose is the current regimen. The 3 g dose and the follow-up dose are older regimens, and a seven-day course is treatment, not prophylaxis."],
  ["A 25 kg child with an unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart defect and no drug allergy needs extraction of 7.5. The correct oral dose of amoxicillin is:","1.25 g",["625 mg","2.5 g","250 mg"],"The child's dose is 50 mg/kg, so 25 kg x 50 mg/kg = 1250 mg. The 2.5 g option exceeds the adult 2 g dose."],
  ["A 20 kg child with a prosthetic pulmonary valve developed urticaria after amoxicillin and needs extraction of 8.5. The most appropriate oral prophylaxis is azithromycin:","300 mg",["150 mg","500 mg","1 g"],"The child's dose is 15 mg/kg, so 20 kg x 15 mg/kg = 300 mg. Urticaria rules out a cephalosporin, and 500 mg is the adult dose."],
  ["A 55-year-old with a mechanical aortic valve had a mild non-urticarial rash with amoxicillin many years ago, with no swelling or breathing difficulty. The most appropriate choice from the following is:","Cephalexin 2 g orally before the procedure",["Clindamycin 600 mg orally before the procedure","Amoxicillin 2 g orally before the procedure","Metronidazole 500 mg orally before the procedure"],"Without anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria, a cephalosporin is acceptable. Clindamycin is the tempting legacy answer, but it was removed in 2021, and metronidazole is not a listed option."],
  ["Clindamycin was removed from the AHA prophylaxis regimens in 2021 chiefly because:","A single dose can lead to severe C. difficile infection and serious reactions",["Viridans streptococci are now universally resistant to it","It does not cross into the bloodstream after an oral dose","It interacts dangerously with all prosthetic valve materials"],"The concern is harm, including fatal C. difficile infection after a single dose. Resistance was not the reason: the AHA cites animal studies in which prophylaxis worked even against strains of variable susceptibility."],
  ["For which of the following patients does the AHA 2021 statement suggest prophylaxis before scaling?","A 68-year-old with a transcatheter aortic valve",["A 45-year-old with mitral valve prolapse and regurgitation","A 60-year-old with a dual-chamber pacemaker","A 52-year-old with rheumatic mitral stenosis, no prosthesis"],"Transcatheter valves count as prosthetic valves. Prolapse and native rheumatic valve disease are not on the list, however much they appear to raise risk, and pacemakers are explicitly excluded."],
  ["A 65-year-old had a drug-eluting coronary stent placed three months ago and needs extraction of 3.7. Regarding endocarditis prophylaxis, the most appropriate decision is:","No prophylaxis, since stents are not on the list",["Amoxicillin 2 g, since the stent is less than six months old","Azithromycin 500 mg, since the patient takes antiplatelet drugs","Delay the extraction until six months after stent placement for infection reasons"],"The AHA lists coronary and other vascular stents as not needing prophylaxis, at any time after placement. The six-month rule applies to repaired congenital defects with prosthetic material."],
  ["A 4-year-old had an atrial septal defect closed with a device three months ago, with complete closure. The child needs extraction of 7.4. Regarding prophylaxis, the most appropriate decision is to:","Give prophylaxis, because the repair is within the first 6 months",["Give none, because septal closure devices are never included","Give prophylaxis for life, because any cardiac device qualifies","Give none, because primary tooth extraction does not qualify"],"Complete repair with prosthetic material is covered during the first 6 months. After that, a completely closed device is not. Extraction clearly manipulates gingival tissue."],
  ["A 58-year-old heart transplant recipient asks whether prophylaxis is needed for periodontal maintenance. Under the AHA 2021 statement, prophylaxis is suggested if the patient has:","Cardiac valvulopathy in the transplanted heart",["Any history of heart transplantation","A current immunosuppressant prescription","A coronary stent placed before transplantation"],"Transplant alone is not the criterion: the listed category is transplant recipients who develop valvulopathy. Immunosuppression is a separate question decided with the treating team."],
  ["A 40-year-old with previous infective endocarditis attends a recall visit. Which procedure calls for prophylaxis under the AHA 2021 statement?","Periodontal probing and scaling",["Bitewing radiographs","Adjusting an orthodontic appliance","Anaesthetic injection through non-infected tissue"],"Probing and scaling manipulate gingival tissue. The other three are specifically listed as not requiring prophylaxis."],
  ["A patient with a mechanical valve forgot her amoxicillin, and the extraction has just been completed. The most appropriate action is to:","Give amoxicillin 2 g now, as it may be given up to 2 hours after",["Give nothing, because a dose after the procedure has no value","Start amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for seven days","Give a dose only if she develops a fever in the following days"],"AHA 2021 allows the dose up to 2 hours after an inadvertently missed pre-procedure dose. A treatment course is not prophylaxis, and waiting for fever misses the window."],
  ["A patient with a prosthetic valve has just finished 7 days of amoxicillin for a sinus infection and has elective scaling booked tomorrow. The preferred approach is to:","Delay the scaling for at least 10 days after the course ended",["Give amoxicillin 2 g tomorrow as usual and proceed","Omit prophylaxis, as the recent course provides sufficient protection","Give clindamycin 600 mg tomorrow and proceed"],"A recent course can select resistant streptococci, so the AHA prefers waiting at least 10 days when the work is elective; if it cannot wait, use a different class. Clindamycin is no longer an option."],
  ["A 70-year-old with a total knee replacement placed six months ago needs extraction of 3.6 and has no cardiac history. According to the Canadian consensus statement, the most appropriate decision is to:","Proceed without antibiotic prophylaxis",["Give amoxicillin 2 g, as the joint is under two years old","Give clindamycin 600 mg, as the joint is a foreign body","Defer the extraction until the joint is two years old"],"COA, CDA and AMMI Canada state that routine prophylaxis is not indicated for joint replacements, and there is no reliable evidence it prevents joint infection. The two-year rule is an outdated idea."],
  ["The orthopaedic surgeon of a patient with a recent hip replacement insists on antibiotics before dental scaling, and you explain the guidance but the surgeon remains firm. According to the ADA, the most appropriate course is that:","The surgeon recommends the regimen and, when reasonable, writes the prescription",["The dentist selects clindamycin, since it reaches bone well","The dentist gives no antibiotic and does not inform the surgeon","The patient chooses an antibiotic from leftover prescriptions at home"],"ADA guidance places this decision and prescription with the surgeon when antibiotics are deemed necessary. Prescribing clindamycin yourself is the tempting but outdated reflex."],
  ["Which of the following best explains why the guidance targets patients at highest risk of adverse outcome rather than all with valve abnormalities?","Benefit is unproven and daily bacteraemia is larger, so a drug's risks are justified only where infection would be most severe",["Patients with native valve disease cannot develop infective endocarditis","All cases of endocarditis follow invasive dental procedures in prosthetic valves","Antibiotics reach only prosthetic material and do not reach native valves"],"Daily activities produce far more bacteraemia than dental visits, and a dose carries real risks, so it is limited to those who would suffer most. Native valves certainly can become infected, so that statement is false."]
 ]
